 "signs": [
  {
   "text": "PULTENEY BRIDGE. Robert Adam, 1774. One of very few bridges in the world lined with shops. Wander over and buy a fudge.",
   "x": 42.0,
   "y": 24.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE ROYAL CRESCENT. 30 houses in one sweeping curve, 1767 to 1774. The lawn drops away in a ha-ha, a hidden ditch that keeps the cows off without spoiling the view.",
   "x": 8.0,
   "y": 14.0
  },
  {
   "text": "BATH ABBEY. A monastery since 675, rebuilt in the 1500s. 'The Lantern of the West' for its huge windows. The ladders carved on the front come from a bishop's dream of angels.",
   "x": 38.0,
   "y": 18.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE ROMAN BATHS. Sulis Minerva's temple, nearly 2,000 years old. About a million litres of 46 degree spring water a day. Please do not drink the green one.",
   "x": 35.0,
   "y": 31.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THERMAE BATH SPA. A rooftop pool of natural hot spring water at about 33 degrees, open to the sky. Britain's only naturally hot springs rise right here in Bath.",
   "x": 56.0,
   "y": 26.0
  },
  {
   "text": "VICTORIA PARK. Opened in 1830 by the 11-year-old Princess Victoria. She was not allowed to run. You are.",
   "x": 23.0,
   "y": 37.0
  },
  {
   "text": "BATH'S HOT SPRINGS. Britain's only natural ones, three of them, rising at the Roman Baths. Cold water from every other tap. Be grateful.",
   "x": 12.0,
   "y": 38.0
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Jane Austen, novelist, lived in Bath from 1801 to 1806, first at 4 Sydney Place.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "bath_austen",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Jane Austen"
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 8,
   "plaque": "bath_circus",
   "title": "The Circus",
   "text": "Up the hill from the Crescent is the Circus, a ring of grand townhouses begun in 1754 by John Wood the Elder and finished by his son. Wood was fascinated by Stonehenge and the Druids, and stone acorns line the roof. Bath does love a circle."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "bath_ralph_allen",
   "title": "Ralph Allen",
   "text": "Ralph Allen made one fortune reforming the postal service and another from his stone mines at Combe Down, south of the city. His limestone built much of Georgian Bath, and he built the mansion of Prior Park on the hillside to show it off."
  },
  {
   "x": 9,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "bath_box_tunnel",
   "title": "Box Tunnel",
   "text": "When Brunel's Great Western Railway opened all the way from London to Bristol in 1841, the last piece was Box Tunnel, east of Bath, nearly two miles long. Legend says the rising sun shines straight through it on 9 April, Brunel's birthday."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 35,
   "plaque": "bath_bladud",
   "title": "King Bladud",
   "text": "Legend says Bladud, a prince with leprosy, was cured by the hot mud here and founded Bath. Geoffrey of Monmouth made him the father of King Lear, and says he died trying to fly over London on wings he had made himself. Ambitious family."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 2,
   "text": "FOOTPATH. North to the Avon Gorge, Clifton and Bristol. Twelve miles, give or take a goat."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "arts_austen_persuasion",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Persuasion",
   "text": "Two of Jane Austen's six novels are set largely in Bath: Northanger Abbey, written when she was young and visiting, and Persuasion, her last, finished in 1816. They were published together in December 1817, five months after she died at Winchester aged 41. Anne Elliot walks these streets in the rain and runs into Captain Wentworth in a shop on Milsom Street. Austen herself was not fond of Bath; the novels are her revenge, and the city has forgiven her completely."
  }
 ],
